-# Design analysis for the combo variant D7/3 + D16/0
-# a) Concrete behavior and trace
-#    - Stage 1 keeps the core b9e38dc0 geometry: detect the wall, infer the opening
-#      direction `rad`, flood the seed inside the funnel, then radiate that fill through
-#      the opening.
-#    - Stage 2 no longer treats extra singleton pixels as shadow-casters. Every extra
-#      non-wall, non-fill singleton is a mover that advances one cell at a time along the
-#      same `rad`.
-#    - If a mover reaches the stage-1 wall+fill sprite, it is absorbed instead of stopping
-#      in front of it. Absorption recolors the whole filled region to that mover's color.
-#      With multiple movers, arrival time is the ray distance to the first hit cell; later
-#      arrivals overwrite earlier recolors, so the last absorber decides the final fill color.
-#    - Small example for `rad = down`: two movers sit above the funnel in different columns.
-#      A near red mover hits after 3 steps, so the filled region becomes red; a farther blue
-#      mover hits after 6 steps, so the same region is recolored blue in the final output.
-#      Any mover whose lane misses the sprite continues to the bottom border as usual.
-# b) Complexity calibration
-#    - The original wall/fill inference is preserved, so the task still has one dominant
-#      large object and one movement direction to read.
-#    - D16/0 is made visible by requiring 2-4 mover singletons per sample, with at least
-#      two absorbed on parallel lanes and at distinct distances. That creates repeated
-#      evidence for "many pixels target one sprite" without introducing multiple competing
-#      pairing rules.
-#    - Optional survivor movers are capped at one or two so movement remains readable and
-#      the core absorption/recolor effect stays central.
-# c) Implicit-assumption audit
-#    - The old shadow logic assumed extra pixels were static blockers. That is invalid now:
-#      extras must be traced as moving entities, and the stage-1 sprite is the obstacle.
-#    - Fill-color detection still assumes the true seed color is the non-wall color closest
-#      to the wall. Generator therefore keeps movers several cells away from the wall.
-#    - The old solver assumed the fill color stays constant after stage 1. D7/3 breaks that:
-#      the solver must preserve a mask of stage-1 filled cells and allow later recoloring.
-#    - Arrival order is a new hidden dependency. Generator guarantees distinct absorber
-#      travel distances so "last arrival wins" is well-defined and visually supported.
-# d) Placement legality
-#    - Movers are placed only on cells that remain background after stage 1, so they never
-#      start inside the wall or inside the initial fill.
-#    - Absorbed movers are chosen from lanes whose straight ray reaches the stage-1 sprite;
-#      survivors, when present, are chosen from lanes that miss the sprite and reach a border.
-#    - Starts are Chebyshev-separated, lanes are distinct, and survivor destinations are
-#      distinct, preventing overlaps and accidental multi-pixel clusters.
-# e) Visual observability
-#    - Input examples visibly contain several isolated pixels upstream of one funnel sprite.
-#      Output examples show those pixels disappearing or reaching the border, while the
-#      funnel's filled area changes to one of their colors.
-#    - Because absorbed movers appear at different distances, humans can infer that the
-#      farther one arrives later and therefore determines the final fill color.
-# f) Visual cleanliness
-#    - The canvas contains one large wall+fill structure plus a small number of singleton
-#      movers. No extra obstacle blocks are added, so ownership stays unambiguous.
-#    - Colors are kept sparse: background, wall, initial fill, and 2-4 mover colors.
-#      The filled region is the only large recolored area, so the output does not become noisy.
-# g) Color inferability
-#    - Color ids are never used numerically. The only color rule is direct transfer:
-#      the filled region adopts the color of the last absorbed mover.
-#    - Generator forces the final absorber color to differ from the initial fill color, so
-#      the transfer is visible rather than silently matching the original fill.
-# 9) New solver assumptions checked against generator
-#    - `_trace_mover` assumes movers travel on unobstructed straight rays and only interact
-#      with the stage-1 sprite or the border. Generator never places other objects on those lanes.
-#    - `main()` assumes absorbed movers are distinguishable by distance if multiple colors
-#      compete to recolor the same fill region. Generator enforces distinct hit distances.
-#    - `main()` assumes extra pixels are singleton movers rather than multi-cell blobs.
-#      Generator uses Chebyshev separation >= 2, so movers stay isolated under 8-connectivity.
+# Design analysis for the combo variant (D7/3 + D16/0).
+# a) Concrete behavior:
+#    Keep the original funnel-wall geometry as stage 1: a seed inside the partial enclosure floods the
+#    reachable pocket and radiates outward through the opening. The new stage 2 objects are multiple
+#    isolated singleton movers associated with that same funnel. They sit behind the closed end on
+#    several parallel lanes and all move in the same radiate direction.
+#    - Original (abstract two-stage reading used for this combo family):
+#      1. Build the wall-defined filled sprite.
+#      2. A singleton mover would continue straight in the same direction, with the stage-1 sprite
+#         merely acting as a blocker.
+#    - Fused rule:
+#      1. Still reconstruct the same stage-1 filled sprite from the wall and the seed.
+#      2. Every extra singleton is a mover. It advances straight along the radiate direction.
+#      3. When its next step would enter the stage-1 sprite, the mover is absorbed instead of stopping
+#         in front of it, and the entire filled region recolors to that mover's color.
+#      4. With multiple movers on different lanes and different travel lengths, the filled region can
+#         recolor several times; the farthest mover arrives last and therefore determines the final
+#         fill color.
+#    Small example: if `rad = up`, three movers can start 3, 4, and 5 cells below the funnel bridge.
+#    They all move upward. The 3-step mover recolors the fill first, the 4-step mover recolors it
+#    again, and the 5-step mover sets the final color.
+# b) Complexity calibration:
+#    The combo stays narrow and legible: one active funnel, 2-3 movers, one absorption type, and one
+#    recolor target (the fill region only, not the wall). Distances vary over a small discrete set so
+#    the "last arrival wins" rule is visible without introducing arbitrary tie logic.
+# c) Implicit assumptions audited:
+#    - Old assumption: extra non-wall colors are static obstacles. Broken; they are now singleton
+#      movers and must be tracked individually.
+#    - Old assumption: stage-2 interaction does not change colors. Broken; absorption recolors the
+#      stage-1 fill region.
+#    - Old assumption: one funnel implies one mover. Broken by D16/0; several movers share the same
+#      wall-derived direction and target.
+#    - New assumption: mover arrival times must be unambiguous. The generator enforces distinct travel
+#      lengths so `main()` never needs a color-id-based tie-break.
+# d) Placement legality:
+#    Movers are placed only on in-bounds background cells behind the funnel bridge, never touching the
+#    wall, never overlapping each other, and always on lanes that hit the stage-1 sprite. The wall is
+#    still a single connected component, the seed stays inside the enclosure, and all placements are
+#    validated by rerunning the solver.
+# e) Visual observability:
+#    Humans can see the rule from geometry alone: the wall still determines the outward direction, the
+#    movers line up behind the same sprite on parallel lanes, they disappear in the output, and the
+#    filled region takes the color of the farthest mover rather than the nearest one.
+# f) Visual cleanliness:
