+# Design analysis for the combo variant (D7/3 + D16/0).
+# a) Concrete behavior:
+#    Keep the original funnel-wall geometry as stage 1: a seed inside the partial enclosure floods the
+#    reachable pocket and radiates outward through the opening. The new stage 2 objects are multiple
+#    isolated singleton movers associated with that same funnel. They sit behind the closed end on
+#    several parallel lanes and all move in the same radiate direction.
+#    - Original (abstract two-stage reading used for this combo family):
+#      1. Build the wall-defined filled sprite.
+#      2. A singleton mover would continue straight in the same direction, with the stage-1 sprite
+#         merely acting as a blocker.
+#    - Fused rule:
+#      1. Still reconstruct the same stage-1 filled sprite from the wall and the seed.
+#      2. Every extra singleton is a mover. It advances straight along the radiate direction.
+#      3. When its next step would enter the stage-1 sprite, the mover is absorbed instead of stopping
+#         in front of it, and the entire filled region recolors to that mover's color.
+#      4. With multiple movers on different lanes and different travel lengths, the filled region can
+#         recolor several times; the farthest mover arrives last and therefore determines the final
+#         fill color.
+#    Small example: if `rad = up`, three movers can start 3, 4, and 5 cells below the funnel bridge.
+#    They all move upward. The 3-step mover recolors the fill first, the 4-step mover recolors it
+#    again, and the 5-step mover sets the final color.
+# b) Complexity calibration:
+#    The combo stays narrow and legible: one active funnel, 2-3 movers, one absorption type, and one
+#    recolor target (the fill region only, not the wall). Distances vary over a small discrete set so
+#    the "last arrival wins" rule is visible without introducing arbitrary tie logic.
+# c) Implicit assumptions audited:
+#    - Old assumption: extra non-wall colors are static obstacles. Broken; they are now singleton
+#      movers and must be tracked individually.
+#    - Old assumption: stage-2 interaction does not change colors. Broken; absorption recolors the
+#      stage-1 fill region.
+#    - Old assumption: one funnel implies one mover. Broken by D16/0; several movers share the same
+#      wall-derived direction and target.
+#    - New assumption: mover arrival times must be unambiguous. The generator enforces distinct travel
+#      lengths so `main()` never needs a color-id-based tie-break.
+# d) Placement legality:
+#    Movers are placed only on in-bounds background cells behind the funnel bridge, never touching the
+#    wall, never overlapping each other, and always on lanes that hit the stage-1 sprite. The wall is
+#    still a single connected component, the seed stays inside the enclosure, and all placements are
+#    validated by rerunning the solver.
+# e) Visual observability:
+#    Humans can see the rule from geometry alone: the wall still determines the outward direction, the
+#    movers line up behind the same sprite on parallel lanes, they disappear in the output, and the
+#    filled region takes the color of the farthest mover rather than the nearest one.
+# f) Visual cleanliness:
+#    Only three layers are visible: wall, fill region, and a few separated singleton movers. Trails are
+#    straight and parallel, and no two movers share a lane or landing time, so the output does not turn
+#    into noise.
+# g) Color inferability:
+#    Color values are never ordered numerically. Structural roles come from shape and proximity:
+#    wall = dominant non-bg component, seed = non-wall color closest to wall, movers = the remaining
+#    singleton extras. Recoloring is always explained by spatial arrival order, not by palette id.
+# h) New solver assumptions checked against the generator:
+#    `main()` assumes every extra component is a singleton mover and that at least two movers are
+#    absorbed on distinct arrival times. `generate_input()` enforces that explicitly and rejects any
+#    sample whose fused output matches a no-absorption straight-to-edge baseline.
 
 
